      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Nikita Pavlichenko"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand which transformer architecture changes actually improve performance under controlled empirical testing. It cuts through research hype by systematically testing and stacking architectural tweaks across over a thousand runs.",
      "hn_id": "50499588",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50499588",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most transformer architectural modifications proposed over the last six years fail to survive rigorous isolated ablation.\n\nA common belief across frontier labs holds that architecture does not matter as much as data quality and post-training recipes. To test this systematically, a comprehensive benchmark ran more than one thousand controlled experiments, isolating attention modifications, normalization styles, and activation functions under identical conditions before stacking the survivors.\n\nThe findings reveal that many celebrated tweaks produce zero practical benefit when controlling for compute and parameter counts. Furthermore, incremental improvements from individual innovations frequently cancel each other out when combined into a single training run.\n\nRigorous ablation prevents infrastructure budgets from being squandered on academic fads."
    },
    {
      "title": "Building a robust execution harness for coding agents",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://vercel.com/academy/build-ai-agent-harness",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Joel Hooks"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the architectural requirements for taking AI coding agents beyond toy demos into real-world work. You will learn how to implement sandboxing, context pruning, and execution safety gates from scratch.",
      "hn_id": "50496396",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50496396",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most attempts to build autonomous coding agents stumble on the harness rather than the reasoning model. An agent tool loop with three basic primitives works well in a quick demo, but running it in production exposes immediate architectural challenges: context windows fill up with thousand-line logs, tool state drifts, and cloud sandbox costs escalate rapidly.\n\nBuilding an effective agent harness requires treating execution safety, filesystem abstractions, and memory lifecycle as core infrastructure. Swapping backends between local child processes and copy-on-write virtual sandboxes must happen behind a single interface without rewriting tool logic. Simultaneously, context management cannot simply dump full terminal transcripts into the prompt; aggressive pruning of command outputs and structured subagent delegation are necessary to prevent catastrophic distraction.\n\nMoving agent logic from prompt experiments to deterministic system harnesses is the defining engineering challenge of current applied AI."
    },
    {
      "title": "Recursive training on generated data causes irreversible model collapse",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17493",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Ilia Shumailov",
        "Zakhar Shumaylov",
        "Yiren Zhao",
        "Yarin Gal",
        "Nicolas Papernot",
        "Ross Anderson"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the mathematical and systemic mechanics behind model collapse caused by recursive synthetic data training. You will learn why uncurated AI-generated content irreversibly degrades model distributions and why genuine human data will become increasingly critical.",
      "hn_id": "50496185",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50496185",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Training modern generative architectures on synthetic or recursive web data introduces a critical failure mode known as model collapse. When generative models ingest data generated by earlier iterations of similar models, statistical tail events disappear progressively until the output distribution narrows completely.\n\nThis phenomenon impacts varied architectures, including Variational Autoencoders, Gaussian Mixture Models, and large language models. Early stages manifest as functional diversity loss across corner cases, followed by late-stage collapse where the network collapses onto an uninformative statistical mean. Because the distribution tails carry low-probability edge conditions and critical reasoning nuances, recursive generation permanently erodes foundational training signal.\n\nHigh-value training runs will increasingly rely on verifiable, genuine human interaction traces rather than self-referential synthetic loops."
    },
    {
      "title": "Writing a DSL for high performance parallel programming in OxCaml",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://blog.janestreet.com/writing-a-dsl-for-high-performance-parallel-programming-in-oxcaml/",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Nicholas Yang"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Jane Street designed an SPMD-based domain-specific language in OxCaml to simplify hardware-level parallel execution. You will learn how modern language features allow high-performance SIMD code to be written ergonomically without sacrificing cross-architecture portability.",
      "hn_id": "50495769",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50495769",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Writing high-performance SIMD code usually forces engineers into an uncomfortable trade-off. You write inner loops in fragile, architecture-specific C intrinsics, and the moment you want to target a newer CPU instruction set or tune vector widths, you must rewrite the code.\n\nJane Street tackled this problem by designing a domain-specific language compiler in OxCaml using an SPMD (Single Program, Multiple Data) execution model. By utilizing runtime metaprogramming features recently introduced into the language, developers write kernels once as standard OxCaml functions and let the compiler generate architecture-optimized vector instructions for CPUs across the firm.\n\nMoving to an SPMD model abstracts away vector registers while retaining bare-metal performance for critical workloads such as low-latency risk checks and options pricing. It proves that raising the abstraction level can yield both hardware portability and extreme throughput without sacrificing execution speed."
    },
    {
      "title": "Safely automating risk decisions with human-in-the-loop agents",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://mays.co/human-in-the-loop-slackbot-agent",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "stevenmays"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how to architect robust human-in-the-loop LLM systems that safely triage compliance decisions across automated rules and human reviewers.",
      "hn_id": "50497322",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50497322",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Automating financial compliance or high-risk business logic with LLM agents sounds great until you realize every subcomponent can and will fail. An unhandled API timeout, a malformed JSON payload, or a sudden spike in token usage can stall operations or inadvertently move funds without proper authorization.\n\nTo manage this risk cleanly, a sensible production pattern isolates operations into three explicit tiers. Clear passes execute through deterministic code alone. Borderline calls on lower-stakes volumes get handed off to an LLM evaluator with exhaustive auditing. Everything else—especially high-value or ambiguous transactions—drops into an asynchronous queue waiting on human approval via Slack.\n\nThe real architectural insight sits in boundary separation. Slack webhooks strictly enforce a tight three-second deadline. To prevent failures, the ingestion layer should merely sign, acknowledge, and push the action into an SQS queue backed by transactional state in Postgres. A decoupled worker Lambda performs the actual Claude API evaluation and downstream Stripe execution.\n\nKeeping non-deterministic reasoning isolated from the critical write path ensures that external outages or hallucinated schemas never corrupt your ledger."
    },
    {
      "title": "Coding agents lack reference monitors to control privileged actions",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://hwtn.io/essays/t0013-missing-ring-0-protections-ai",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "danielberanek"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why current AI coding assistants operate with unchecked system authority and how external reference monitors can mitigate prompt injection risks.",
      "hn_id": "50498251",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50498251",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Current autonomous coding agents like Claude Code, Codex, and Gemini CLI operate with an alarming security assumption: whatever the model generates, the host OS executes with full user permissions. Because these tools ingest untrusted codebases, issues, and third-party web content, they remain inherently vulnerable to prompt injection attacks.\n\nThe core structural vulnerability is that today's security validations live inside individual agent tools rather than in a unified external supervisor. When a specialized file-editing tool rejects an unsafe patch, the agent can simply bypass the check by writing a payload via the generic terminal shell.\n\nClassic operating system architecture solved this dilemma decades ago with reference monitors. A reference monitor guarantees complete mediation, meaning every single action must pass through an immutable, external gatekeeper before affecting the host state. Instead of trusting agent reasoning, systems require an out-of-band commit boundary that validates diffs against deterministic policies before applying them.\n\nTreating agent execution as untrusted user space instead of a privileged kernel process is the only sustainable way to run autonomous software systems safely."
    },
    {
      "title": "Managing quantization error in end-to-end four-bit training",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/end-to-end-4-bit-training/",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Jack Cook"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how modern hardware handles the precision tradeoffs of four-bit numerical representations during model training. You will learn the mechanics behind formats like NVFP4 and MXFP8 and how to balance speed against rounding error accumulation.",
      "hn_id": "50498252",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50498252",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Training large models in 4-bit precision has long been considered impractical because rounding errors compound rapidly across backward passes. New hardware architectures like NVIDIA Grace Blackwell Ultra (GB300) are changing that equation with native NVFP4 support, delivering up to six times higher throughput than BF16 matrix operations.\n\nThe real engineering challenge lies in balancing precision formats across different stages of the computation graph. NVFP4 uses an average of 4.5 bits per value and delivers fifteen PFLOPS, but unchecked quantization error destabilizes optimizer states. Combining NVFP4 with MXFP8 for sensitive tensor operations provides a viable pathway to retain accuracy while slashing memory footprints.\n\nHardware-aware quantization is quickly becoming the primary lever for cutting training cluster footprints in half."
    },
    {
      "title": "GPT-6 Astra executes verification techniques with genuine effectiveness",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://danluu.com/astra-testing/",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "ksec"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how modern AI agents evolve from superficial test generation to executing structured, effective software verification.",
      "hn_id": "50497749",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50497749",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Instructing coding agents to follow formal verification techniques often degrades their output instead of improving it.\n\nDan Luu evaluated frontier models on implementing complex protocols like the Zstd and IMAP specifications from raw RFCs. On earlier iterations, forcing agents to use test-driven development, property testing, or Lean resulted in caricatures of those techniques. The agents generated superficial unit tests or aimless fuzzing inputs that covered the same trivial paths, costing more money while reducing overall correctness compared to unconstrained generation.\n\nWith newer reasoning models, capability jumps dramatically saturate these benchmarks. Implementing the Zstd specification dropped in cost from 3.50 dollars to 1.00 dollar while test correctness surged from 63 percent to over 99 percent. More importantly, when asked to fuzz, the newer model constructed structured inputs that exercised non-trivial edge cases rather than spitting out garbage data.\n\nPrescribing engineering dogmas to an autonomous agent is counterproductive until the model possesses the underlying reasoning capacity to execute them properly."
    },
    {
      "title": "Tparquet halves time series file sizes using pcodec",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/aperiodic-io/tparquet",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Aperiodic"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how tparquet integrates the pcodec numeric compression codec into standard Parquet to cut time series storage in half. It demonstrates how to store financial tick data efficiently without breaking ecosystem compatibility.",
      "hn_id": "50497648",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50497648",
      "image": "/infographics/13-github-50469022.jpg",
      "content": "Standard columnar formats like Apache Parquet work well across generic analytic workloads, but they hit real efficiency limits when handling high-frequency numerical time-series. Generic compression algorithms such as ZSTD leave substantial redundancy on the table when compressing tick data.\n\nTParquet addresses this by integrating Pcodec directly into the Parquet specification as a dedicated compression codec for numeric columns. Across 139 million trades from four market data providers, it achieved a 0.49x compression ratio relative to standard ZSTD while remaining fully lossless.\n\nThe real architectural advantage is compatibility. The underlying file preserves standard Parquet metadata, row groups, page indexes, and statistics, allowing integrations with DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion, and Trino to read the data without custom file formats.\n\nOptimizing at the codec layer delivers massive disk and network savings without breaking existing analytical ecosystems."
    },
    {
      "title": "Morletdb enables shift-tolerant waveform archive search in postgresql",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/merkhet-io/morletdb",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "zy4"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how MorletDB extends PostgreSQL to store raw sensor recordings losslessly and perform shift-tolerant envelope similarity searches.",
      "hn_id": "50500544",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50500544",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most time-series databases treat sensor metrics as isolated scalar points, which falls short when analyzing complex analog waveforms like vibration or acoustics. MorletDB takes a different route by extending PostgreSQL via Rust and pgrx to treat raw sensor recordings as first-class, lossless artifacts.\n\nInstead of decimating samples or relying on crude point-in-time aggregations, the engine implements a shift-tolerant envelope search directly inside SQL. It indexes and searches raw continuous signals for pattern similarity, returning the exact sample intervals alongside native calibration and acquisition metadata. It also exposes this search capability over the Model Context Protocol (MCP), allowing AI agents to query physical waveforms natively.\n\nPushing signal processing and envelope similarity algorithms straight into the Postgres storage engine eliminates huge data transfer overheads. It provides a clean, unified architecture for teams managing high-frequency sensor telemetry without maintaining detached vector stores."
    },
    {
      "title": "Preference-based model evaluations are vulnerable to manipulation",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14769",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Eve Fleisig",
        "Joachim Baumann",
        "Dylan Hadfield-Menell",
        "Dirk Hovy"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how arena-style language model leaderboards can be systematically manipulated using strategic voting, prompting, and nomination. You will learn the mathematical mechanisms that enable these vulnerabilities and how to design safeguards against them.",
      "hn_id": "50499650",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50499650",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Crowdsourced, preference-based arena evaluations have become the gold standard for comparing frontier language models. However, new research proves that Bradley-Terry aggregation models are fundamentally vulnerable to systemic manipulation.\n\nThe paper demonstrates that adversarial actors can exploit ranking mechanics through three distinct attack surfaces: strategic prompting, targeted voting, and strategic nomination. By deliberately voting on carefully selected pairs of unrelated models or injecting synthetic competitors, an entity can artificially lift a model by 15 to 20 leaderboard positions. Combined attacks managed to shift rankings by as much as 45 spots on public evaluation data.\n\nFor engineering teams selecting models based on public arena scores, this research exposes a serious reliability problem. Blindly trusting pairwise aggregation without strict anomaly filtering and prompt diversification guarantees flawed architecture decisions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Serving Kimi K3 requires specialized distributed GPU infrastructure",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.digitalocean.com/blog/serving-kimi-k3-inference-engine",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Jonathan Dieu",
        "Shree Murthy"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the precise hardware requirements and distributed serving architecture needed to run the trillion-parameter Kimi K3 model. You will gain insight into managing extreme memory footprints across heterogeneous GPU clusters.",
      "hn_id": "50502297",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50502297",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Deploying a massive mixture-of-experts model on day zero reveals the harsh mechanical realities of modern inference infrastructure.\n\nDigitalOcean published an operational post-mortem detailing how they stood up Moonshot AI Kimi K3, a model with 2.78 trillion total parameters across 896 routed experts. The model interleaves 69 Kimi Delta Attention layers with 24 Gated Multi-head Latent Attention layers. The raw weights alone consume 1.56 terabytes, requiring a baseline of 195 gigabytes of VRAM per GPU before allocating a single megabyte to the key-value cache.\n\nTo make this workable, the deployment architecture standardizes on eight-way NVIDIA HGX B300 and AMD Instinct MI350X systems equipped with 288 gigabytes of memory per accelerator. They relied on an open runtime called llm-d to handle heterogeneous execution across both hardware vendors without maintaining divergent serving stacks.\n\nWhen models scale past single-node limits, memory layout and interconnect bandwidth dictate your entire software architecture."
    },
    {
      "title": "AI assistants struggle to address misconceptions behind user requests",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/xybench",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Kyle Wiggers"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why current language models struggle to diagnose the real intent behind misguided queries and how the XYBench benchmark evaluates root-cause problem solving.",
      "hn_id": "50495693",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50495693",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most LLM assistants suffer from a fundamental product flaw: they answer the question you asked rather than solving the problem you have. When a user asks how to thicken cake frosting that is sliding off hot cupcakes, frontier models happily provide frosting thickeners instead of pointing out that the cakes need to cool down first.\n\nXYBench, which received the Best Resource Paper Award at EMNLP, introduces an 8,000-question benchmark specifically evaluating whether models catch the implicit misconception behind a query. Evaluated against Claude Sonnet, GPT-5, Gemini 3.1 Pro, and DeepSeek, every single model repeatedly failed to steer users toward the correct solution. Instead, the models anchored heavily on the user's mistaken premise and executed the sub-optimal request.\n\nFor engineers building autonomous agents, this evaluation highlights an architectural gap in current reasoning traces. Giving an agent tool-calling access without an explicit validation and intent-clarification stage ensures that bad instructions propagate directly into production environments.\n\nTrue agentic intelligence requires questioning erroneous premises before executing the plan."
    },
    {
      "title": "Balancing vocabulary quotas enables efficient multilingual tokenization",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://trius.canton.graphics/blog/000003.html",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "TriusAI"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how tokenizers can allocate fixed vocabulary budgets across diverse scripts to avoid costly byte fallback while maintaining English efficiency.",
      "hn_id": "50497748",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50497748",
      "image": null,
      "content": "When standard BPE algorithms run over common crawl data, English and Chinese dominate the token vocabulary. Non-Latin scripts like Arabic, Cyrillic, Greek, and Devanagari are routinely pushed into multi-byte fallback mode. In Hindi or Greek, words often consume three to four tokens per character, shrinking effective context windows by up to 75 percent and forcing attention layers to assemble raw bytes instead of semantic tokens.\n\nTriusLD solves this by introducing per-script quotas under a rigid 65,535 token ceiling. Instead of giving twenty thousand slots to Unihan or letting raw web frequency skew the distribution, it enforces balanced representation across 19 writing systems while retaining a shared byte fallback.\n\nThe compression results are striking: 0.52 tokens per character across 58 languages, compared to 0.84 for GPT-4 vocabulary tables, with only an 18 percent penalty on English density relative to a 200,000-token model. Keeping the total vocabulary strictly within a 16-bit addressable range avoids the significant linear embedding layer memory tax incurred by modern frontier models.\n\nOptimizing your tokenizer vocabulary allocation is often the highest-leverage way to reduce context inflation without altering your model architecture."
    },
    {
      "title": "Burrmill replaces DuckDB for sealed Parquet query execution",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/nuthatch-org/burrmill",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "cargopete"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Burrmill replaced DuckDB in production to achieve faster analytical queries with exact integer arithmetic. It demonstrates the trade-offs of building a pure-Rust engine tailored specifically for sealed Parquet segments.",
      "hn_id": "50497375",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50497375",
      "image": null,
      "content": "DuckDB has become the default embedded analytical engine for querying Parquet files, but pulling large C++ codebases into strict Rust production pipelines introduces substantial build friction and subtle numeric edge cases. In workloads like financial ledgers or blockchain indexers, silent numeric wrap-around is unacceptable.\n\nBurrmill addresses this by implementing an analytical SQL engine in pure Rust that queries sealed Parquet segments alongside a live memory tip. Crucially, it replaces standard numeric overflow behavior with an explicit refuse-on-overflow strategy, throwing errors rather than silently wrapping or coercing into floating-point numbers.\n\nOn real-world indexing views, the engine achieved byte-identical outputs to DuckDB while executing warm queries in 0.65x of the time. The authors accepted a slightly larger binary footprint and marginally slower per-statement ad-hoc latency in exchange for eliminating C++ dependencies and ensuring strict arithmetic safety across their storage nodes.\n\nWhen evaluating embedded SQL engines, choosing domain-tailored arithmetic constraints often yields better reliability than relying on generic analytical defaults."
    },
    {
      "title": "Before trusting an agent you must break its box",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/11/06/before-i-trust-an-agent/",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Kunal Kushwaha"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how to rigorously stress-test AI agent isolation boundaries using virtual machines and host-side verification. You will learn practical techniques for probing containment limits and verifying security without relying on guest reporting.",
      "hn_id": "50498175",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50498175",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Running an autonomous agent with shell access directly on developer machines or shared cloud hosts is an operational disaster waiting to happen. An agent ingesting untrusted prompts, web pages, or dependency trees will inevitably execute malicious instructions. Sandboxing cannot simply rely on the guest process reporting its own confinement.\n\nA robust defense architecture enforces one dedicated lightweight virtual machine per agent, anchored by Apple Virtualization framework on macOS or KVM on Linux. The host keeps all credentials, API keys, and network routing policies strictly outside the guest memory space.\n\nValidating this isolation boundary requires treating the agent environment as fully compromised from root. In rigorous penetration runs, an attacker in the guest attempted tens of thousands of race conditions against exposed host ports and dropped symlinks pointing at host secrets across shared mounts. Host-side packet witnesses and cryptographic file hashing verified that zero connections crossed the hypervisor boundary.\n\nRelying on software wrappers or container cgroups is not enough when agents execute dynamic code. True containment requires hardware-assisted hypervisor boundaries verified by external host witnesses rather than guest logs."
    },
    {
      "title": "Formalizing tensor layout selection as combinatorial optimization",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21555",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [
        "Clemens Eisenhofer",
        "Yuwen Jia",
        "Daniel Kroening",
        "Sergey Pupyrev"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand the computational complexity of tensor memory layout selection and how formulating it as a combinatorial optimization problem enables optimal compiler planning.",
      "hn_id": "50500144",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50500144",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Modern machine learning compilers spend significant execution time converting tensor memory layouts between connected operations. When a matrix multiplication prefers row-major layout but a downstream convolution demands channel-last packing, naive heuristics trigger costly layout conversions that waste device memory bandwidth.\n\nResearchers have formalized this global layout selection problem for the first time as combinatorial optimization over dataflow graphs. While the general problem is computationally intractable even for basic matrix multiplications, dataflow graphs with bounded treewidth admit an exact polynomial-time solution. For broader graph topologies, encoding the objective function into a weighted MaxSAT solver generates optimal layout schedules across unified hardware pipelines such as XLA and systolic array targets.\n\nHardware efficiency in machine learning comes down to layout planning rather than raw compute alone."
    },
    {
      "title": "Hacker News Story",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=5049236",
      "date": "2026-11-14",
      "authors": [],
      "why_read": "Learn how token-level classification removes prompt bloat in RAG pipelines without slow autoregressive summarization.",
      "hn_id": "5049236",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=5049236",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most retrieval-augmented generation pipelines choke on noise. Injecting twenty raw chunks into a large model wastes context windows, dilutes attention, and explodes inference latency. Standard summarization filters often introduce extra autoregressive latency, which defeats the purpose.\n\nChopper uses a tiny 0.31B encoder (based on mmBERT) that treats RAG filtering as an extractive classification task instead of a text generation problem. The encoder processes the user question and twenty candidate snippets in a single pass, classifying each token as keep or drop in roughly 50 milliseconds on standard hardware.\n\nBy avoiding text generation, downstream models receive exact quotes without hallucination risks or secondary LLM latency loops. In benchmarks, chaining four-hop questions from a single extraction pass yielded accuracy comparable to large frontier models while stripping out redundant prompt bulk.\n\nFiltering retrieved tokens with specialized tiny encoders is vastly superior to relying entirely on brute-force context windows."
